<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Polotsk</h1><p class="st-meta">987 CE – 1392 CE (approx.) · capital Polotsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The oldest principality of the Belarusian lands, a Dvina trading power that kept its own dynasty and long resisted Kiev.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Western Dvina</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krivichs">Krivich</a> town ruled in the 10th century by the Scandinavian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogvolod">Rogvolod</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir I</a> killed him and married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogneda-of-polotsk">Rogneda</a>. Around 987 Vladimir gave Polotsk to their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izyaslav-of-polotsk">Izyaslav</a>, whose descendants held it as a separate dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Dvina</a> route to the Baltic and traded with Riga and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gotland">Gotland</a>. Its princes often fought the rest of Rus', and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vseslav-of-polotsk">Vseslav</a>, called the Sorcerer, who ruled for over fifty years, even held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> briefly in 1068. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sophia-cathedral-polotsk">Cathedral of Saint Sophia</a> and the abbess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrosyne-of-polotsk">Euphrosyne of Polotsk</a> made it a religious centre.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the 12th century the land split into smaller principalities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, and German crusaders took its Livonian tributaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> in the early 14th century, and its separate princely rule ended in 1392 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> installed his own governors.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-polotsk.jpg" alt="Principality of Polotsk" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Alex Tora · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alex_K_Polotsk_general.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>987 CE – 1392 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Polotsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rogvolod, Izyaslav of Polotsk, Vseslav of Polotsk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Belarus, Latvia, Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Polatsk Principality</dd></dl></aside></div>
